Interview Answer (2 Minutes)

Deployment is used for stateless applications where pods are interchangeable and can be scaled easily. StatefulSet is used for stateful workloads like databases that require stable identities, persistent storage, and ordered deployment. DaemonSet is used when a pod must run on every node in the cluster, such as CNI plugins, monitoring agents, and log collectors.

Easy Memory Trick
Plain Text
Deployment = Many identical app pods
 
StatefulSet = Database pods with identity + storage
 
DaemonSet = One pod on every node

For Kubernetes mid-to-senior interviews, remember:

Plain Text
Nginx/API Server → Deployment
MySQL/Kafka/MongoDB → StatefulSet
Cilium/Fluentd/Node Exporter → DaemonSet

The root network namespace is the default Linux network namespace associated with the host node. Physical interfaces (eth0/ens5), routing tables, iptables rules, bridges, and eBPF programs typically exist in this namespace. Kubernetes creates a separate network namespace for each Pod and connects it to the root namespace using veth pairs through a CNI plugin such as Cilium, Calico, or AWS VPC CNI. All Pod-to-Pod and Pod-to-external traffic ultimately traverses the host's root network namespace.
